 Chow: 1 point\n\nThat gives me a total of 11. To get Mixed Triple Chow I need [W4, W5, W6], [B4, B5, B6], [T4, T5, T6] tiles. I already have [W4, W5, W6] and [B4, B5, B6], so now I need [T4, T5, T6]. I have T4, T5 so I am 1 T6 away from that achievement. The probability of getting T6 is high.\n\n**W8 vs. B2: Decision Time**\n\nSo now I have a final decision to make: keep W8 or B2?\n\nLet's look at it.\n\n*   **W8:** Not contributing to Mixed Triple Chow. Has the potential to form W7-W8 (if W7 is drawn).\n*   **B2:** Not contributing to Mixed Triple Chow. Neighbors B1, B3.\n\nI'd rather hold onto the possibility of W7 for W7-W8 and discard B2. But since T6 is available it's going to be better to just focus on the hand I have.\n\n**Final Decision**\n\nNow considering everything, I will *discard B2*. I'm going to discard B2 because my plan is to get a mixed triple chow (8 points) to win the game. Discarding B2 helps me achieve that and gives me a better hand.\n\n\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand includes a completed chow of Characters [W4, W5, W6], and tiles in Dots [B2, B4, B5, B6, B6] that can form a chow [B4, B5, B6] and a pair [B6, B6] (or a single B6 if the pair is not formed yet). The Bamboos [T4, T5] form a side wait for T3 or T6. The most promising scoring pattern is \"Mixed Triple Chow\" (8 points) consisting of W4-W5-W6, B4-B5-B6, and T4-T5-T6. With 4 T6 tiles unshown, this is a highly achievable goal that meets the 8-point minimum. The tiles W8 and B2 are isolated and not essential to this plan. Comparing W8 and B2, B2 is less efficient because the tiles required to connect with it (B1 and B3) have fewer unshown copies (B1: 1, B3: 2) compared to W8's neighbors (W7: 2, W9: 2). Additionally, discarding B2 helps clear the hand for All Simples.\n\nReason: The hand is 2-shanten for a Mixed Triple Chow (W456, B456, T456) which is worth 8 points. W8 and B2 are isolated tiles. B2 is chosen for discard because it has poorer connectivity potential than W8 